From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Egor Kulikovskiy <xianel0x@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 01:40:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] x9: milestone checkpoints from arm_init into kernel_bootstrap

Hardware readback with 0187 proved arm_vm_init completes on X9: the record ended
at SWEET EARLY 14 and the reset had fired from sweet_early_late_init, which only
runs after arm_vm_init returns. The "hang" was an artifact of where the checkpoint
sat, not a kernel fault. Later attempts carried the reset further and each one
named the milestone that ran last:

  stage 21  -> pstore mapped / early log flushed
  stage 24  -> patch_low_glo, sleep-token setup, PE_consistent_debug_inherit
  stage 30  -> serial_init returned, fbcon mapping done
  stage 35  -> kernel_startup_initialize_upto(KPRINTF) returned
  stage 36  -> kprintf returned
  stage 49  -> arm_init.c calls machine_startup

Move the reset out of sweet_early_late_init into sweet_diag_checkpoint, so one
place owns it, and route every arm_init.c milestone through that function.
SWEET_X9_CHECKPOINT_STAGE picks the milestone that resets.

The reset has to come from the kernel. A candidate that hangs can only be
recovered with a forced restart, and that is a cold reset: DRAM is reinitialized
and the record is gone before Android can snapshot it. A build that merely
recorded milestones and kept running was tried for exactly this reason and left
no record at all -- console-ramoops-0 was absent on the next boot. Reset-at-a
-stage therefore stays the only usable channel, and one attempt can only ever
show the milestones up to the chosen one.

49 -> 60 was a blank window on hardware: stage 49 resets, stage 50 does not, and
the two are separated by one call. Milestones 50-56 split that window into its
actual steps:

  50 machine_startup entered        54 kernel_bootstrap entered
  51 fastipi parsed                 55 version printed
  52 machine_conf returned          56 scale_setup returned
  53 calling kernel_bootstrap       60 vm_mem_bootstrap (first bootstrap stage)

Milestone 50 answers that question with sweet_diag_entry_reset(): a bare
compare and SMC at machine_startup()'s first statement, with no call, no stack
frame, no record access and no sweet_early_mark() -- the mark path renders
glyphs into the framebuffer, which is exactly what must be ruled out at a
function entry. A build that stops at 50 therefore leaves a record ending at
49's checkpoint label with no "diag:" line, and its reset proves
machine_startup() was entered. sweet_diag_probe() (the same reset, but it
appends to the record when one is mapped) stays available for milestones where
a written line is wanted.

Past arm_init the same mechanism covers kernel_bootstrap() stages, now numbered
60-71. kernel_bootstrap() already funnels every stage through
kernel_bootstrap_log() and kernel_bootstrap_thread_log(), so one hook in each
covers all 40+ stages instead of a mark at every call site. The stage is
identified by name, not by call order: kernel_bootstrap_log() is inlined at 40+
sites and is also reached from the startup-entry loop, so a counter would depend
on inlining and drift between builds. Names are mapped to fixed milestone
numbers and unknown names are ignored, which keeps the record to the stages worth
naming. This is why the mark cap moves to SWEET_X9_MAX_STAGE (96).

sweet_diag_checkpoint() is noinline on purpose. Inlined into
sweet_bootstrap_mark(), the comparison against SWEET_X9_CHECKPOINT_STAGE
compiled to a test against zero instead of 60..71, because the stage arrives from
a table the compiler cannot fold; the branch could then never fire and a
bootstrap checkpoint would silently never reset. Disassembly of the built object
is what exposed it, not a warning.

A checkpoint refuses to touch the record before it is mapped, because milestones
1-15 run out of local buffers.

Off by default through 0187's SWEET_X9_DIAG_RESET; on a working kernel it turns
every boot into a reset loop.
An earlier attempt to walk this window one milestone at a time was far slower than
it should have been, for a reason that turned out to be in the instrumentation:
sweet_early_disable() runs right after the kprintf milestone, and from then on
sweet_early_mark() returns without writing anything. The record therefore always
ended at the last mark before the disable -- a build that reset at 49 showed its
final line as "SWEET EARLY 20" -- so each attempt could only say "reset happened,
last visible mark was 20" and not which checkpoint fired. sweet_diag_checkpoint()
now appends the label straight to the persistent record when the mark path is
disabled, so every checkpoint proves itself and one boot shows how far execution
actually got.

sweet_diag_entry_reset() is the last resort for the same question: a bare compare
and SMC with no call, no stack frame and no record access, used at the first
statement of machine_startup(). If a normal checkpoint never fires there, this
separates "the function was entered" from "the checkpoint machinery failed".

